        "\n",
        "# Chapter 21. Information Theory and Language\n",
        "\n",
        "Information theory gives a mathematical language for uncertainty, prediction, compression, and dependence.\n",
        "For natural language processing, these ideas are not optional background.\n",
        "They appear directly in language-model training, perplexity, tokenization, decoding, representation learning, retrieval, and alignment.\n",
        "\n",
        "The central observation is simple:\n",
        "\n",
        "> A language model assigns probabilities to possible tokens and sequences.  Therefore, a language model can be studied using the mathematics of probability distributions and information.\n",
        "\n",
        "Earlier chapters used cross-entropy as a loss function and perplexity as an evaluation metric.\n",
        "This chapter explains why those quantities are natural.\n",
        "The goal is to make explicit the connection between prediction and compression:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{good probabilistic prediction} \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow \\quad \\text{short code length on average}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For MA Applied Mathematics and MS Statistics students, information theory is especially useful because it gives clean definitions that apply to both classical statistical NLP and modern neural language models.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip}\n",
        "## Guiding principle\n",
        "Entropy measures uncertainty under a true distribution.\n",
        "Cross-entropy measures the average loss when one distribution is used to predict another.\n",
        "KL divergence measures the extra cost caused by using the wrong distribution.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 21.1 Learning goals\n",
        "\n",
        "After studying this chapter, you should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. compute entropy, cross-entropy, KL divergence, and perplexity for discrete token distributions;\n",
        "2. explain why cross-entropy is the standard training loss for language models;\n",
        "3. interpret perplexity as an exponential average branching factor;\n",
        "4. use the entropy chain rule to decompose uncertainty in a sequence;\n",
        "5. define conditional entropy and mutual information;\n",
        "6. connect pointwise mutual information with word co-occurrence and embeddings;\n",
        "7. explain how tokenization changes entropy and code length;\n",
        "8. interpret KL regularization in fine-tuning and alignment;\n",
        "9. implement information-theoretic quantities from scratch in Python;\n",
        "10. critique language-model evaluations using information-theoretic reasoning.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 21.2 Discrete distributions over tokens\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $V=\\{1,2,\\ldots,d\\}$ be a finite vocabulary.\n",
        "A token distribution is a vector\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p=(p_1,\\ldots,p_d) \\in \\Delta^{d-1},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_j \\ge 0, \\qquad \\sum_{j=1}^d p_j=1.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Here $\\Delta^{d-1}$ is the probability simplex.\n",
        "In NLP, $p_j$ may represent the empirical frequency of token $j$ in a corpus, the conditional probability assigned by a language model to token $j$, or the predicted class probability in a classifier.\n",
        "\n",
        "Given counts $c_1,\\ldots,c_d$ from a corpus, the empirical distribution is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat p_j = \\frac{c_j}{\\sum_{r=1}^d c_r}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is the maximum likelihood estimator for a categorical distribution.\n",
        "It is also the simplest bridge between text and information theory: a corpus becomes a point in a probability simplex.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## Logarithm base\n",
        "If logs use base $2$, entropy is measured in bits.\n",
        "If logs use base $e$, entropy is measured in nats.\n",
        "Machine learning usually uses natural logarithms because gradients are simpler.\n",
        "This chapter mostly uses natural logarithms.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 21.3 Entropy\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $X$ be a discrete random variable with distribution $p$ on $V$.\n",
        "The entropy of $X$ is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "H(X)=H(p)=-\\sum_{j=1}^d p_j \\log p_j.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "By convention, $0\\log 0=0$.\n",
        "Entropy measures the average uncertainty before observing $X$.\n",
        "Equivalently, it is the average information content of the outcome:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "H(X)=E[-\\log p_X].\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The quantity\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "I(x)=-\\log p(x)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "is called the surprisal or self-information of the event $X=x$.\n",
        "Rare events have high surprisal; common events have low surprisal.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Basic properties\n",
        "\n",
        "For a distribution on $d$ outcomes,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "0 \\le H(p) \\le \\log d.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The lower bound occurs when all probability mass is concentrated on one outcome.\n",
        "The upper bound occurs at the uniform distribution $p_j=1/d$.\n",
        "Thus entropy is largest when all tokens are equally likely.\n",
        "\n",
        "This upper bound is easy to prove using KL divergence.\n",
        "Let $u_j=1/d$ be the uniform distribution.\n",
        "Because $D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(p\\|u)\\ge 0$,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "0 \\le \\sum_{j=1}^d p_j\\log\\frac{p_j}{1/d}\n",
        "= \\sum_{j=1}^d p_j\\log p_j + \\log d.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "H(p) = -\\sum_{j=1}^d p_j\\log p_j \\le \\log d.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The inequality says that no distribution on $d$ tokens has more uncertainty than the uniform distribution.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 21.4 Cross-entropy and the log score\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose the true token distribution is $p$, but a model predicts distribution $q$.\n",
        "The cross-entropy of $p$ relative to $q$ is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "H(p,q) = -\\sum_{j=1}^d p_j \\log q_j.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "It is the expected negative log-probability assigned by $q$ when the data are generated from $p$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "H(p,q)=E_{X\\sim p}[-\\log q_X].\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is exactly the population version of the language-model loss.\n",
        "Given tokens $x_1,\\ldots,x_n$, the empirical cross-entropy loss is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat H(p,q) = -\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n \\log q_{x_i}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If $q=q_\\theta(\\cdot \\mid \\text{context})$ is a neural language model, then minimizing cross-entropy is the same as maximizing likelihood.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Why the negative log score?\n",
        "\n",
        "The negative log score has several special properties.\n",
        "It rewards assigning high probability to the observed token, but it also punishes overconfident mistakes severely.\n",
        "If $q_x$ is close to zero for the observed token $x$, then $-\\log q_x$ is very large.\n",
        "\n",
        "For one-hot target vector $e_y$ and predicted distribution $q$, the cross-entropy loss is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell(q,y)=-\\sum_{j=1}^d (e_y)_j\\log q_j=-\\log q_y.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is the loss used in next-token prediction.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 21.5 KL divergence\n",
        "\n",
        "The Kullback--Leibler divergence from $q$ to $p$ is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(p\\|q)=\\sum_{j=1}^d p_j\\log\\frac{p_j}{q_j}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "It is not symmetric, so it is not a metric.\n",
        "However, it has a crucial interpretation:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(p\\|q) = H(p,q)-H(p).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus KL divergence is the extra expected log loss incurred by predicting with $q$ when the true distribution is $p$.\n",
        "Since $H(p)$ does not depend on $q$, minimizing cross-entropy over $q$ is equivalent to minimizing KL divergence from the data distribution to the model distribution.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Gibbs inequality\n",
        "\n",
        "For distributions $p$ and $q$ with $q_j>0$ whenever $p_j>0$,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(p\\|q)\\ge 0,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "with equality if and only if $p=q$.\n",
        "One proof uses the concavity of $\\log$.\n",
        "Let $Z=q_X/p_X$ when $X\\sim p$.\n",
        "Then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "-D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(p\\|q)\n",
        "= \\sum_j p_j\\log\\frac{q_j}{p_j}\n",
        "\\le \\log\\left(\\sum_j p_j\\frac{q_j}{p_j}\\right)\n",
        "=\\log\\left(\\sum_j q_j\\right)=0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore $D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(p\\|q)\\ge 0$.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-warning}\n",
        "## Support mismatch\n",
        "If $p_j>0$ but $q_j=0$, then $D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(p\\|q)=\\infty$.\n",
        "This is the information-theoretic version of the zero-probability problem in n-gram language models.\n",
        "Smoothing is necessary because a model should not assign zero probability to plausible future tokens.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 21.6 Perplexity\n",
        "\n",
        "For a sequence of $n$ observed tokens $x_1,\\ldots,x_n$, suppose a language model assigns conditional probabilities\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "q_i = q_\\theta(x_i\\mid x_{<i}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The average negative log-likelihood is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{NLL}_{\\text{avg}} = -\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n \\log q_i.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The perplexity is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{PPL}=\\exp\\left(\\operatorname{NLL}_{\\text{avg}}\\right).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "When natural logarithms are used, perplexity is the exponential of average cross-entropy in nats.\n",
        "When base-$2$ logarithms are used,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{PPL}=2^{H_2},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $H_2$ is measured in bits.\n",
        "\n",
        "Perplexity can be interpreted as an average branching factor.\n",
        "A perplexity of $20$ means the model is, in an information-theoretic sense, as uncertain as if it were choosing uniformly among about $20$ equally likely tokens at each step.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## Perplexity depends on tokenization\n",
        "Perplexity is not directly comparable across different tokenizers.\n",
        "A character-level model, word-level model, and subword-level model define different random variables.\n",
        "Changing the tokenization changes the unit of prediction and therefore changes the entropy scale.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 21.7 Sequence entropy and the chain rule\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $W_{1:T}=(W_1,\\ldots,W_T)$ be a sequence of tokens.\n",
        "The joint entropy is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "H(W_{1:T})=-\\sum_{w_{1:T}}p(w_{1:T})\\log p(w_{1:T}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The entropy chain rule states that\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "H(W_{1:T})=\\sum_{t=1}^T H(W_t\\mid W_{1:t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This identity matches the autoregressive factorization\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p(w_{1:T})=\\prod_{t=1}^T p(w_t\\mid w_{1:t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Taking negative logs and expectations gives the entropy chain rule.\n",
        "Thus autoregressive language modeling is not just an engineering trick.\n",
        "It is aligned with the fundamental decomposition of uncertainty in a sequence.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Entropy rate\n",
        "\n",
        "For a stationary stochastic process $W_1,W_2,\\ldots$, the entropy rate is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\bar H = \\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{T}H(W_{1:T}),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "when the limit exists.\n",
        "Equivalently, under suitable conditions,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\bar H = \\lim_{T\\to\\infty}H(W_T\\mid W_{1:T-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For language, the entropy rate measures the long-run average uncertainty per token after using all previous context.\n",
        "Better context modeling should reduce conditional entropy.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 21.8 Conditional entropy and mutual information\n",
        "\n",
        "For random variables $X$ and $Y$, the conditional entropy is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "H(Y\\mid X) = -\\sum_x p(x)\\sum_y p(y\\mid x)\\log p(y\\mid x).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "It measures the remaining uncertainty in $Y$ after observing $X$.\n",
        "The mutual information between $X$ and $Y$ is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "I(X;Y)=H(Y)-H(Y\\mid X).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "It is also\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "I(X;Y)=\\sum_{x,y}p(x,y)\\log\\frac{p(x,y)}{p(x)p(y)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "I(X;Y)=D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(p(x,y)\\|p(x)p(y)).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Mutual information measures how far the joint distribution is from independence.\n",
        "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then $p(x,y)=p(x)p(y)$ and $I(X;Y)=0$.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Mutual information in NLP\n",
        "\n",
        "Mutual information appears naturally in several places:\n",
        "\n",
        "- word co-occurrence: how much does seeing one word tell us about a nearby word?\n",
        "- classification: how informative is a token about a label?\n",
        "- representation learning: how much information does an embedding retain about input or target variables?\n",
        "- retrieval: how informative is a document about a query?\n",
        "- prompting: how much does an instruction reduce uncertainty about the desired answer?\n",
        "\n",
        "## 21.9 Pointwise mutual information and word co-occurrence\n",
        "\n",
        "For two events $X=x$ and $Y=y$, the pointwise mutual information is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{PMI}(x,y)=\\log\\frac{p(x,y)}{p(x)p(y)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "PMI is positive when $x$ and $y$ co-occur more often than expected under independence.\n",
        "It is negative when they co-occur less often than expected.\n",
        "\n",
        "In word-context analysis, we may let $X$ be a center word and $Y$ be a context word.\n",
        "Then PMI becomes\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{PMI}(w,c)=\\log\\frac{p(w,c)}{p(w)p(c)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The positive part is called positive PMI:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{PPMI}(w,c)=\\max\\{\\operatorname{PMI}(w,c),0\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Chapter 8 used PPMI matrices for word embeddings.\n",
        "This chapter explains why the ratio is meaningful: it compares observed co-occurrence with what independence would predict.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 21.10 Compression interpretation\n",
        "\n",
        "Information theory connects probability and coding.\n",
        "If an event $x$ has probability $p(x)$, an ideal code assigns it length approximately\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell(x)=-\\log_2 p(x)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "bits.\n",
        "Frequent events receive shorter codes; rare events receive longer codes.\n",
        "The expected code length is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "E[\\ell(X)] = H_2(X).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If we encode data generated from $p$ using a code designed for $q$, the expected code length becomes\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "H_2(p,q)=H_2(p)+D_{\\mathrm{KL},2}(p\\|q).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus KL divergence is the excess number of bits per token caused by using the wrong probability model.\n",
        "This gives a concrete interpretation of language-model loss:\n",
        "\n",
        "> Lower cross-entropy means the model gives a shorter average code for the text.\n",
        "\n",
        "This viewpoint is useful for understanding why language modeling is such a general pretraining task.\n",
        "To compress text well, a model must learn spelling, syntax, semantics, style, factual regularities, and long-range dependencies.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 21.11 Tokenization and information\n",
        "\n",
        "Tokenization changes the random variable being modeled.\n",
        "Suppose the same text can be represented as characters, words, or subwords.\n",
        "Each representation gives a different sequence:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "C_{1:m}, \\qquad W_{1:n}, \\qquad S_{1:r}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The entropy per token is not directly comparable across these representations because the token units are different.\n",
        "A word-level model may have a large vocabulary and high entropy per token.\n",
        "A character-level model may have a small vocabulary and lower entropy per token, but many more tokens per sentence.\n",
        "\n",
        "A more meaningful comparison is often code length per original character or per original byte:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{bits per character} = \\frac{\\text{total code length in bits}}{\\text{number of characters}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is why compression-based comparisons must specify the coding unit.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 21.12 KL divergence in fine-tuning and alignment\n",
        "\n",
        "In Chapter 18, we saw objectives that include a KL penalty.\n",
        "A common form is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\max_\\theta E_{y\\sim \\pi_\\theta(\\cdot\\mid x)}[r(x,y)]\n",
        "-\\beta D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(\\pi_\\theta(\\cdot\\mid x)\\|\\pi_0(\\cdot\\mid x)).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Here $\\pi_0$ is a reference model and $\\pi_\\theta$ is the fine-tuned model.\n",
        "The reward term encourages helpful behavior.\n",
        "The KL term discourages the new model from moving too far from the reference distribution.\n",
        "\n",
        "Mathematically, the KL penalty is a distributional trust region.\n",
        "It does not simply penalize parameter distance.\n",
        "Two models may have very different parameter vectors but similar output distributions, or similar parameters but different output distributions.\n",
        "KL regularization measures distance in probability behavior.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 21.13 Information bottleneck viewpoint\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $X$ be an input sequence, $Z$ be a learned representation, and $Y$ be a target.\n",
        "The information bottleneck principle asks for a representation $Z$ that is useful for predicting $Y$ but compressed relative to $X$.\n",
        "One idealized objective is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\min \\ I(X;Z) - \\beta I(Z;Y).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The first term encourages compression: $Z$ should not retain irrelevant details of $X$.\n",
        "The second term encourages prediction: $Z$ should retain information about $Y$.\n",
        "\n",
        "This viewpoint is not a complete theory of large language models, but it provides useful language for asking mathematical questions:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Which information about the input is preserved in an embedding?\n",
        "- Which information is discarded by pooling, truncation, or retrieval?\n",
        "- How does fine-tuning change the information available for a downstream task?\n",
        "- How can we measure whether a representation contains syntactic, semantic, or factual information?\n",
        "\n",
