        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 21.22 Common mistakes\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Comparing perplexity across tokenizers.**  Perplexity depends on the prediction unit.\n",
        "2. **Treating KL divergence as a distance metric.**  KL is asymmetric and does not satisfy the triangle inequality.\n",
        "3. **Forgetting support conditions.**  If $q_j=0$ where $p_j>0$, cross-entropy and KL divergence are infinite.\n",
        "4. **Confusing entropy and cross-entropy.**  Entropy uses the true distribution inside both the expectation and the logarithm.  Cross-entropy uses the true distribution in the expectation and the model distribution in the logarithm.\n",
        "5. **Overinterpreting PMI for rare events.**  PMI can be large for very rare co-occurrences.  Smoothing and minimum-count thresholds are often needed.\n",
        "6. **Ignoring logarithm base.**  Natural logs give nats.  Base-$2$ logs give bits.\n",
        "7. **Assuming lower entropy always means better language.**  Entropy measures uncertainty under a distribution, not semantic quality by itself.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 21.23 Worked calculation\n",
        "\n",
        "Let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p=(0.5,0.3,0.2), \\qquad q=(0.4,0.4,0.2).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "H(p)=-0.5\\log 0.5-0.3\\log 0.3-0.2\\log 0.2.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Numerically,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "H(p) \\approx 1.0297.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The cross-entropy is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "H(p,q)=-0.5\\log 0.4-0.3\\log 0.4-0.2\\log 0.2.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Numerically,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "H(p,q)\\approx 1.0559.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(p\\|q)=H(p,q)-H(p)\\approx 0.0262.\n",
